DISC = '''The readout rises because the two motor pools share more of their timing, not because either fires more. Most of the effect survives pairing traces from different runs (193 of 240 pairs), and in the continuously driven segment the carrier-removed network is the one arm of the scan that keeps the structure in half its seeds (§2.3). That is why we describe the readout as antiphase duty and not as endogenous motor organisation: a network that is merely more reproducibly locked to a shared stimulus passes the same test. The shared stimulus is the phase of the external drive. Shifting the tone by half a period in one run of a pair, at identical gates, inverts the deletion's cross-seed effect, and no elevation survives when the partner run is undriven (§2.5).

The two pools are not affected alike. On the base they peak together against the drive. With the carrier removed the extensor's peak moves by about a third of a period while the flexor's holds, so their relation rotates partway toward antiphase without reaching it (§2.5). The deletion acts on the timing of one pool of the pair. We offer this as a description and not a mechanism: which input sets the extensor's phase once the carrier is gone is not known.

'''
